##############

- **Talk says:** anyone who has done a roguelike development tutorial has seen this one [[t:45-46]].
- **Talk says:** keep adding rooms until there are enough by your own criteria [[t:50]].
- **Demo:** a fixed 20 attempts. A rectangle that overlaps is discarded without another try [[c01:20-54]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=6

## Controls
@@control attempts
Rectangles tried. The demo: a fixed 20, and a discarded one is not tried again [[c01:20-54]].
@@control min_size
Smallest value a room’s width and height are drawn from [[c01:22-27]]. A room covers 1 tile more than the value each way: the demo’s rectangle type includes both of its ends [[own]].
@@control max_size
Largest such value. The demo writes it as an upper end of 10, which its random call leaves out [[own]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

- **Random rooms:** {{c01.regions.max}} region and {{c01.dead_ends.max}} dead ends on every seed [[run:c01.regions.max c01.dead_ends.max]].
  - **Why:** each room is joined to the one before it, and a corridor ends inside a room [[own]].
- **Rooms placed:** of 20 attempts, {{c01.rooms.median}} at the median; never fewer than {{c01.rooms.min}} [[run:c01.rooms.median c01.rooms.min]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

- **No loop without an end:** attempts are a fixed 20; corridors, one for each room after the first [[c01:20-21 c01:67-78]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

- **As a base map:** the talk’s demos of erosion and of a prefab placed in a room both start by building random rooms [[c08:21-83 c14:21-125]].

